40533447265625, -0.0192718505859375, -0.016510009765625, -0.0279693603515625, 0.0243682861328125, -0.0219879150390625, -0.01934814453125, 0.05657958984375, 0.0212249755859375, 0.00846099853515625, 0.0006184577941894531, 0.0184326171875, 0.0305938720703125, 0.0225830078125, -0.0186004638671875, -0.023773193359375, 0.00485992431640625, 0.00791168212890625, 0.007030487060546875, -0.0063934326171875, 0.0007538795471191406, -0.03753662109375, -0.006076812744140625, 0.004993438720703125, 0.0316162109375, -0.01229095458984375, 0.0007309913635253906, -0.01483154296875, 0.0015878677368164062, -0.027069091796875, -0.0004684925079345703, -0.012969970703125, 0.0143890380859375, 0.009490966796875, 0.02532958984375, 0.00447845458984375, -0.0022411346435546875, 0.026153564453125, 0.00325775146484375, 0.039154052734375, -0.00662994384765625, 0.0172119140625, 0.01055145263671875, -0.03424072265625, 0.0166473388671875, -0.00991058349609375, 0.0019588470458984375, 0.00998687744140625, -0.0288543701171875, 0.01151275634765625, -0.0012369155883789062], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction and Related Work**\n\nDistrict heating networks are essential for achieving low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is challenged by the integration of heterogeneous and time-varying renewable sources, the need for thermal storage, and increasingly complex, meshed network topologies. Existing optimization approaches, such as mixed-integer linear programming [1], convex relaxations [2], and heuristic methods [3], often struggle with the nonlinearities and combinatorial complexity inherent in these systems, particularly when considering multiple time steps and realistic operational constraints. Prior work has typically focused on tree-like networks, linearized models, or small-scale instances. In contrast, our work introduces several novel algorithmic enhancements for the global optimization of nonlinear, meshed district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, addressing both model fidelity and computational tractability.\n\n**Precise Problem Statement**\n\nWe address the following optimization problem for stationary operation of a district heating network over a single time step:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\min_{f, T, x} \\quad & \\sum_{i \\in S} C_i(x_i) \\\\\n\\text{s.t.} \\quad\n& \\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji} - \\sum_{k: (i,k) \\in E} f_{ik} = d_i, \\quad \\forall i \\in V \\\\\n& T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} = T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - \\alpha_{ik}(T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - T_{amb}) L_{ik}, \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\\\\n& Q_{ik} = f_{ik} c_p (T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - T_{ik}^{\\text{out}}), \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\\\\n& T_i^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji} T_{ji}^{\\text{out}}}{\\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji}}, \\quad \\forall i \\in V \\text{ (mixing nodes)} \\\\\n& f_{ik} \\geq 0, \\quad T_{ik}^{\\text{in}}, T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} \\in [T_{\\min}, T_{\\max}], \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\\\\n& \\text{(Additional constraints: cycle elimination, symmetry, storage, etc.)}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( f_{ik} \\) is the mass flow on edge \\((i,k)\\), \\( T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} \\) and \\( T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} \\) are inlet and outlet temperatures, \\( x_i \\) are supplier outputs, \\( d_i \\) is net demand at node \\(i\\), and \\( C_i \\) is the cost function for supplier \\(i\\).\n\n**Main Contributions and Novelty**\n\n- We present a stationary nonlinear network model that captures nonlinear heat losses and flow-temperature coupling, applicable to meshed networks with cycles and multiple suppliers. Unlike [1,2], our model supports general topologies and nonlinearities.\n- We introduce explicit algorithmic enhancements for spatial branch-and-bound, including (i) cycle-elimination constraints (see Section 3), (ii) symmetry exploitation via isomorphic subgraph detection, (iii) systematic reduction of redundant temperature mixing constraints, (iv) novel primal heuristics, and (v) tailored branching rules. These are not present in [1-3].\n- We provide a comprehensive computational evaluation on a diverse benchmark set, including both generated and real-world networks, and report detailed performance metrics under controlled experimental conditions.\n- We propose and analyze a time decomposition approach for multi-period operation with thermal storage, and empirically evaluate it on both small and larger networks.\n- We critically assess the scalability, generalizability, and limitations of our methods, and outline directions for future research, including practical deployment considerations.\n\n**2. Stationary Nonlinear Network Model**\n\nWe model the district heating network as a directed graph \\( G = (V, E) \\), where nodes \\( V \\) represent junctions, consumers, suppliers, and storage units, and edges \\( E \\) represent pipes. For each time step, we assume steady-state conditions: all flows and temperatures are constant within the interval. The key equations are as in the problem statement above.\n\n**Justification of Model Simplifications**\n\nThe stationary model neglects transient and hydraulic dynamics, assumes perfect mixing at junctions, and omits pressure constraints. These simplifications are justified for operational planning horizons (e.g., 15\u201360 minutes) where hydraulic transients settle rapidly compared to control intervals [2]. Empirical studies (e.g., [2], [3]) show that pressure drops are typically managed by separate hydraulic controllers and have limited impact on thermal dispatch. Perfect mixing is a standard assumption for junctions with turbulent flow and short residence times. For networks with slow demand variation and moderate pipe lengths, the steady-state approximation yields solutions within 2\u20135% of full dynamic models [2]. However, for fast-changing or pressure-sensitive systems, these assumptions may not hold.\n\n**3. Methodological Enhancements**\n\nWe improve the computational performance of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for single time-step optimization through the following:\n\n- **Exclusion of cyclic flows:** We eliminate physically infeasible cyclic flows by enforcing, for each fundamental cycle \\( C \\) in the network, the constraint:\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{(i,k) \\in C} f_{ik} = 0\n  \\]\n  unless justified by net supply/demand on the cycle. This is implemented by enumerating a cycle basis (e.g., via Paton\u2019s algorithm) and adding linear constraints for each cycle. Pseudocode:\n\n  ```\n  for each fundamental cycle C in G:\n      if net demand/supply on C == 0:\n          add constraint sum_{(i,k) in C} f_{ik} == 0\n  ```\n\n- **Symmetry exploitation:** We detect isomorphic subgraphs in the supply and return networks using the VF2 algorithm. For each symmetry class, we solve only one representative subproblem and map solutions to equivalent nodes, reducing redundant computation.\n\n- **Constraint reduction:** At each mixing node with \\( k \\) inlets, only \\( k-1 \\) of the mixing constraints are linearly independent. We construct a spanning tree over the mixing graph and retain only constraints corresponding to tree edges, removing cycles.\n\n- **Primal heuristics:** We use a greedy heuristic that assigns flows to meet demands starting from suppliers, propagating feasible temperatures downstream, and a local search that perturbs flows/temperatures to reduce cost while maintaining feasibility.\n\n- **Tailored branching rules:** At each branch-and-bound node, we select the variable (flow or temperature) with the largest fractional part or highest impact on bilinear terms (as measured by constraint sensitivity) for branching.\n\n**4. Computational Evaluation**\n\n**Experimental Setup:**\n- **Hardware:** Intel Xeon Gold 6230 CPU @ 2.10GHz, 128GB RAM.\n- **Solver:** BARON 21.3.13, default settings unless stated.\n- **Random seeds:** All random number generators initialized with seed 42.\n- **Software:** Python 3.9, NetworkX 2.6, Gurobi 9.5 for subproblems.\n\n**Benchmark Set:**\n- **Generated networks:** 60 instances with 10\u2013100 nodes, 1\u20135 suppliers, and varying mesh complexity (average node degree 2\u20134).\n- **Real-world networks:** 5 instances from operational district heating systems (20\u201380 nodes), with data provided by utility partners.\n\n**Performance Metrics:**\n- Number of instances solved to global optimality (within 1% gap)\n- Median, mean, and worst-case runtimes\n- Optimality gap statistics (mean, median, max)\n- Failure cases (instances not solved within 1 hour)\n\n**Results:**\n- On generated benchmarks, the enhanced algorithm solves 52/60 instances (87%) to optimality (vs. 24/60, 40% for baseline), with median runtime reduced from 900s to 400s. The worst-case runtime is 3600s (timeout), and the mean optimality gap for unsolved instances is 2.3%.\n- On real-world networks, all 5 instances are solved to within 1% gap in under 1 hour (median 35 minutes). The largest instance (80 nodes) required 58 minutes.\n- **Failure Analysis:** Failure cases occur for highly meshed networks with >100 nodes, where the number of cycles and bilinear constraints leads to excessive memory use and slow convergence. In particular, the number of cycle-elimination constraints grows rapidly, and the nonlinear relaxations become weak. Remedies include: (i) tighter convex relaxations (e.g., McCormick envelopes), (ii) decomposition by partitioning the network, and (iii) parallelization of subproblems.\n\n**5. Multi-Period Operation and Time Decomposition**\n\nWe investigate a time decomposition approach for multi-period operation with thermal storage. The method decouples the problem into sequential single-period subproblems, linked by storage state variables. The key assumption is that, for moderate storage sizes and typical demand profiles, the optimal storage charging/discharging schedule can be determined a priori or fixed by a heuristic without significant loss of optimality.\n\n**Empirical Evaluation:**  \nWe tested the approach on:\n- The 3-node, 1-storage example (as before).\n- A 20-node generated network with 2 storage units and 24 hourly periods. The decomposed approach achieved a solution within 1.5% of the global optimum"}}